Context pruning (clean-room)
----------------------------
Loam's verbatim context pruning (src/agent/prune/, src/tools/contextRecallTool.ts)
is an independent implementation of the publicly described idea of dropping
low-value whole messages instead of paraphrasing them. No upstream code was copied.

Project wiki and handoff (clean-room)
-------------------------------------
Loam's per-project wiki (src/wiki/, src/tools/wikiTools.ts) is an independent
implementation of the publicly described idea of a git-friendly markdown
knowledge base with session summaries and handoffs between sessions. No
upstream code was copied.

App bridges (clean-room)
------------------------
Loam's app bridges (src/bridges/, src/tools/appBridgeTools.ts) are an independent
implementation of the publicly described idea of driving desktop applications
through their command-line interfaces with declarative manifests. No upstream
code was copied.

Security audit (src/security/audit/)
------------------------------------
Loam's source-code security audit is a clean-room rewrite of the publicly described
approach of the Cloudflare security-audit-skill (MIT License, Copyright Cloudflare, Inc.):
a coverage-led, multi-phase audit with an independent verification pass. No upstream
code was copied; the rule files in rules/loam/ are Loam's own.

External scanners are invoked as separate command-line processes if installed on the
user's machine and are not bundled or redistributed by Loam:
  - Gitleaks (MIT License)
  - OSV-Scanner (Apache License 2.0)
  - GuardDog (Apache License 2.0)
  - Opengrep (LGPL 2.1), run as a separate process with Loam's own rules
  - Trivy (Apache License 2.0), filesystem scans only
  - Nuclei (MIT License), only against an explicitly configured live target
  - garak (Apache License 2.0, NVIDIA), external Python process for agent
    self-audit (Phase G) only, never against customer code

Native security scanners (src/security/native/)
-----------------------------------------------
Clean-room implementations; no upstream source was copied.
  secrets/  design follows Gitleaks (MIT): keyword prefilter, regex + entropy,
            allowlists, redaction, SARIF output. The default rule set is
            Gitleaks' config/gitleaks.toml (MIT), vendored verbatim at
            rules/loam/secrets/gitleaks.toml and translated for JS RegExp at
            load time; Loam-authored rules win on id collisions.
  sca/      matches against the OSV database (osv.dev, Apache-2.0 / CC-BY-4.0 data)
            and follows OSV-Scanner's lockfile coverage (Apache-2.0).
  pkgintel/ heuristics follow the ideas in DataDog GuardDog (Apache-2.0):
            typosquatting, install scripts, bundled binaries, risk scoring.
  lleval/   prompt-evaluation core follows promptfoo's design (MIT):
            deterministic assertion handlers returning {pass, score, reason},
            a model-graded rubric and vars expansion. No upstream code was copied.
  fuzz/     planning and triage follow Jazzer.js (Apache-2.0): CLI flags, exit
            codes and bug-detector report titles. The fuzzer itself runs as an
            external process and is not bundled or redistributed by Loam.

